WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.649
Hi, I'm Puffin!

00:00:02.649 --> 00:00:08.085
Ling-3.0-flash is a hundred-and-twenty-four-billion-parameter open model that aims at a million-token context.

00:00:08.085 --> 00:00:11.257
Here's the stack that makes that cheap.

00:00:12.808 --> 00:00:16.966
The idea: five of every six attention layers keep a fixed-size memory

00:00:16.966 --> 00:00:18.698
instead of a growing cache.

00:00:18.698 --> 00:00:21.469
The sixth keeps full attention, for exact recall.

00:00:22.958 --> 00:00:24.712
A token climbs seven groups.

00:00:24.712 --> 00:00:28.571
Each is five linear attention blocks, then one full attention block.

00:00:28.991 --> 00:00:30.814
The five are Kimi Delta Attention.

00:00:30.814 --> 00:00:34.459
Instead of a cache that grows every token, each keeps a fixed-size

00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:35.675
state and rewrites it.

00:00:36.095 --> 00:00:40.176
The sixth is gated latent attention: real full attention with a compressed

00:00:40.176 --> 00:00:43.917
cache, so the forgetful layers have something exact to anchor to.

00:00:44.337 --> 00:00:47.659
Every block ends in five hundred and twelve experts.

00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:52.089
Eight fire per token, plus one shared expert, so each token uses

00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:53.935
only about five billion parameters.

00:00:55.608 --> 00:00:57.928
Here's what a KDA layer does with each token.

00:00:58.348 --> 00:01:01.670
First it erases a little: a learned gate decays the old

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:02.878
state, channel by channel.

00:01:03.298 --> 00:01:05.181
Then it writes the new key and value.

00:01:05.181 --> 00:01:06.123
That's the delta rule.

00:01:06.928 --> 00:01:11.294
The state never grows, so these layers run in linear time and constant memory.

00:01:12.758 --> 00:01:17.932
On inclusionAI's own coding numbers, it edges the field on SWE-Bench Pro,

00:01:17.932 --> 00:01:19.657
including its one-trillion-parameter sibling.

00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:21.813
Treat those as vendor numbers.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.572
Cheap, forgetful layers plus one exact layer in six: most of full

00:01:27.572 --> 00:01:30.387
attention's fidelity, at a fraction of its memory.

00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:35.600
To recap: a fixed-size state in five layers, one exact layer in

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.822
six, and eight experts out of five hundred and twelve.

00:01:39.242 --> 00:01:41.325
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.325 --> 00:01:41.920
I'm Puffin.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.218
Bye!
